---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import editor from '../../assets/img/heygen-editor.png';
import onePage from '../../assets/img/heygen-one-page.png';
import layoutRight from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-1.png';
import layoutSidebar from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-4.png';
import timelineBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-timeline-before.png';
import timelineAfter from '../../assets/img/heygen-timeline-after.png';
import playbarFloating from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-floating.png';
import playbarNone from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-none.png';
import playbarRow from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-row.png';
import panelError from '../../assets/img/heygen-panel-error.png';
import panelHappy from '../../assets/img/heygen-panel-happy.png';
import iosConsent from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-consent.png';

// Every frame is a region of one of the Webflow case page's own images, cut so that the deck's
// annotations stay out of the picture: the image shows, the label names, the words argue. The
// shipped editor has the first screen to itself; the one page it replaced sits with the chapter
// that argues against it; the play bar's three states are one rect — the band between canvas
// and timeline, the seam at the same line in each — stacked on the words' column. Each fact is
// said once, where it does the most work; the page ends on the reversal, in one line.
const eaton = {
  when: '2022.06 – 2022.08', org: 'Eaton Corporation', role: 'UX Design Intern', title: 'RapidLinkX',
  sub: 'An IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems, with parameter editing and error monitoring.',
  to: '/projects/eaton',
};
---
<Case
  title="Heygen — Kyle Xu"
  description="Product Design Intern at Heygen, June–October 2023. The AI video editor re-architected around the script with an updated component library — engagement +32.1%, task completion +24.3% against the flow it replaced — then a first iOS companion MVP for avatar creation, delivered in a three-week sprint."
  ogImage="/og-heygen.jpg"
  eyebrow="Heygen · Product Design Intern · June – October 2023"
  deck="Customers were making multi-scene videos in an editor built for one script on one page. I rebuilt it around the script: the text in the sidebar, a timeline that shows time, and a play bar I had to put back."
  next={eaton}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">The video editor, <span class="nb">re-architected</span> around the script.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" src={editor} evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    alt="The Heygen video editor as shipped: two script cards in a dark panel on the left, an avatar on a white canvas, and below them a transport row and a timeline with a ruler and playhead"
    caption="The editor as shipped: the script in the left sidebar, a transport row between canvas and timeline, the current time on the playhead." />

  <section class="col sec" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>The desktop editor — script panel, timeline, component library — as its only designer; at the end, the first iOS companion for recording an avatar.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Shipped</dt>
        <dd>The editor with its updated component library; the iOS MVP stayed a prototype.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>
          <ul class="nums">
            <li><b>+32.1%</b><span>engagement</span></li>
            <li><b>+24.3%</b><span>task completion</span></li>
          </ul>
          <span class="line">Measured on the new editor against the <span class="nb">one-page</span> flow it replaced.</span>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-hard">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-hard">Why one page stopped working</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p><span class="nb">Text-to-speech</span> was still the core of the product, so whatever replaced the page had to give the script more room, not less — and the page had been built to have nothing to learn, which still mattered. Product and engineering read that scope differently, and I was the only designer on it, so I proposed a <span class="nb">one-week</span> design sprint to get everyone to one starting layout. Three decisions came out of it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={onePage} evidence="Shipped"
        alt="The old editor's working panel: a script box with one sentence in it, a voice card with a speed slider and a Play Scripts button, and beneath them a timeline holding a play button, a single scene card and a plus"
        caption="The page as it was: one script box, one voice, and a timeline of one card with a play button and no ruler." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-script">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-script"><span class="n">1</span>Where the script lives</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The timeline needed the bottom of the page, which freed the script's old spot. I drew four layouts and they came down to two ideas. A <span class="nb">right-hand</span> panel was easy to extend but moved the script to the far edge, one panel among several. The sidebar — a tab beside Template, Avatar, Text, Element and Asset — gave it the most room and moved nothing else.</p>
      <p>I chose the sidebar. The sheet's own cons line called its extensibility mediocre, and it was, but an existing user could open the new editor and still know where they were.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="pair" evidence="Concept" caption="Two of the sprint's four layouts, the script in orange."
        items={[
          { src: layoutRight, note: 'A right panel, turned down', alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar on the left, canvas in the middle, script and action panels stacked on the right, timeline along the bottom' },
          { src: layoutSidebar, note: 'The sidebar, chosen', alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar and script together on the left, canvas in the middle, timeline along the bottom, nothing else' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-timeline">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-timeline"><span class="n">2</span>A timeline that shows time</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The old timeline was a row of cards and a play button. My first pass gave it three tracks — avatar, scenes, script — and a mark every fifteen seconds, and that is what we put in front of agencies and individual creators. People weren't sure what they had selected, and a scene's place in time was still a guess between the marks. A full <span class="nb">multi-track</span> suite was never on the table.</p>
      <p>What shipped reads as time first: a ruler every ten seconds with finer ticks, the current time on the playhead, transport buttons and an expand toggle above the tracks, stronger state cues on the cards. Each panel got a visible boundary, and whatever appears in two panels stays in sync, so a scene selected in the timeline lights up on the canvas.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack" caption="The timeline before and after the usability test, cut from the same editor."
        items={[
          { src: timelineBefore, name: 'Before testing', evidence: 'Prototype', alt: 'The first timeline: a play button, then three rows of cards for the avatar, the script and the scenes, with a mark at 0, 15, 30 and 45 seconds' },
          { src: timelineAfter, name: 'After testing', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The shipped timeline: an expand toggle, transport buttons, a ruler marked every ten seconds, a playhead reading 00:38:88, and the avatar, scene and script tracks in a fixed order' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-playbar">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-playbar"><span class="n">3</span>The play bar, three times</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>In the first round I drew a play bar floating over the canvas. It didn't get through — too much on the canvas — so I went the other way and proposed dropping it: the timeline had a playhead and a play button of its own, and one control fewer would read as simpler.</p>
      <p>That's the version we tested, and people kept reaching for the bar that wasn't there. Previewing is constant in this tool, fix a line and play it back, and a play button down in the timeline sat too far from where they were looking. Engineering had run into trouble with that version too. I put the play bar back, as a slim row between canvas and timeline.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack" narrow caption="The band between canvas and timeline, cut the same way in each state."
        items={[
          { src: playbarFloating, name: 'Floating', note: 'round one, turned down', evidence: 'Concept', alt: 'The band between canvas and timeline, with a play bar floating over the lower edge of the canvas: play, a progress line, 00:00 of 00:30, zoom' },
          { src: playbarNone, name: 'None', note: 'round two, my proposal', evidence: 'Concept', alt: 'The same band with no play bar: the canvas ends, and the timeline begins with a lone play button beside its tracks' },
          { src: playbarRow, name: 'A row', note: 'after the test', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The same band with a slim row between canvas and timeline: previous, play, next, and zoom controls' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-shipped">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-shipped">Into engineering</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The editor went across as two flow maps, Text to Script and Timeline Editor, with each edge case drawn as its own state: an empty script, a generation error, a voice that can't take a pitch setting, a script over the word limit. With a small team the maps doubled as the review list, and the updated component library went with them. Against the <span class="nb">one-page</span> flow it replaced, the new editor lifted engagement 32.1% and task completion 24.3%.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="pair" evidence="Supporting state" caption="Empty card and generation error, beside the happy path."
        items={[
          { src: panelError, alt: 'The script panel with a filled card, an empty card reading Please type your script text, a pause, a recording, and a red banner at the bottom reading Script generation error' },
          { src: panelHappy, alt: 'The script panel on the happy path: two filled script cards with a character count, a five-second pause and a recording' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-ios">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-ios">Then, a phone</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Avatar creation was <span class="nb">desktop-only</span> and its weakest link was the webcam, so in the last three weeks I led the first iOS MVP for recording one: scope set with the CPO and product team, 10+ user interviews, six internal evaluation rounds, a <span class="nb">mid-fidelity</span> prototype. Its one real decision is on this screen — consent is a sentence read to the camera in the same session, so the footage carries its own authorisation. Early validation was positive; against Heygen's other initiatives it showed less growth potential and was indefinitely postponed. A prototype that works is not, by itself, a case for building the product.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={iosConsent} phone evidence="Prototype"
        alt="A phone screen with the camera on the presenter and a consent script beneath: I, Kuanghong Xu, hereby declare that I authorize HeyGen to use the footage of me to build a HeyGen avatar"
        caption="The consent screen: the sentence, then Record Again or It Looks Good." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="stance" slot="close">The best decision in this project is the one I reversed.</p>
</Case>
